  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $F,$ then ${A}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{2}.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}_{1}+{A}_{2})[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & ={A}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{A}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{1}+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]{A}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]({A}_{1}+{A}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $F,$ then $A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A.$ For $k\\in R,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kA)[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & =k\\cdot A[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}](kA).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $kA$ is a matrix from $F.$ So, $F$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $F$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"",
